[" Here's a basic implementation of the `RealTimeRandomGenerator` class in C++ using the Mersenne Twister algorithm, which provides high-quality randomness while maintaining reasonable performance. The class is thread-safe using a mutex to protect access to the internal state.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <mt19937ar>
#include <random>
#include <mutex>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <vector>

namespace SimulationUtils {

class RealTimeRandomGenerator {
public:
    RealTimeRandomGenerator(unsigned long long seed, std::function<std::vector<double>(void)> externalDataSource)
        : generator_(seed), dataSource_(externalDataSource), mutex_(), condition_(), isUpdating_(false) {}

    ~RealTimeRandomGenerator() { stop(); }

    double next() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        while (isUpdating_) {
            condition_.wait(lock);
        }
        auto rngState = generator_.extract(mt19937ar::state_type::get_state());
        double result;
        if (dataSourceCalled_) {
            result = dataSource_[currentDataIndex_];
            currentDataIndex_ = (currentDataIndex_ + 1) % dataSource_.size();
            dataSourceCalled_ = false;
        } else {
            generator_.seed(rngState);
            result = generator_.generate();
        }
        lock.unlock();
        return result;
    }

    void update() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        dataSourceCalled_ = true;
        condition_.notify_one();
        lock.unlock();
        std::thread([this]() {
            auto data = dataSource_();
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
            isUpdating_ = false;
            for (auto& d : data) {
                generator_.seed(d);
            }
            lock.unlock();
        }).detach();
    }

    void stop() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        isUpdating_ = true;
        condition_.notify_all();
        lock.unlock();
    }

private:
    std::mt19937ar generator_;
    std::function<std::vector<double>(void)> dataSource_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable condition_;
    bool isUpdating_{false};
    bool dataSourceCalled_{false};
    size_t currentDataIndex_{0};
};

} // namespace SimulationUtils
```

This implementation uses the Mersenne Twister algorithm for generating random numbers and a mutex to ensure thread safety. The `next()` method retrieves the next random number, while the `update()` method updates the internal state with data from an external source. The `stop()` method is used to gracefully stop the RNG when it's no longer needed.

The class handles occasional network latencies by buffering the external data in a vector and updating the RNG state as soon as new data becomes available. The frequency of updates can be adjusted based on the specific requirements of your application."]

[' To achieve this task, I'll provide you with an example of a C# method using Dapper, a popular micro-ORMapper for .NET, which simplifies working with databases like PostgreSQL. This method should meet most of your requirements, but it's essential to adapt and optimize it according to your specific use case and system constraints.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Data;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using Dapper;
using Npgsql;

public class QueryBuilder
{
    private readonly IDbConnection _connection;

    public QueryBuilder(IDbConnection connection)
    {
        _connection = connection;
    }

    public IDataReader ExecuteDynamicQuery(string query, Dictionary<string, object> parameters, string sortBy = null, bool ascending = true, int pageSize = 25, int pageIndex = 0)
    {
        var sql = new StringBuilder();
        var sbParams = new StringBuilder();

        sql.Append(query);

        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(sortBy))
        {
            sql.Append(" ORDER BY ");
            sql.Append(sortBy);
            sbParams.Append('@');
            sbParams.Append(GetParameterName(sortBy));
            sql.Append(" ");
            if (ascending)
                sql.Append("ASC");
            else
                sql.Append("DESC");
        }

        var parametersCollection = parameters.ToList();
        foreach (var parameter in parametersCollection)
        {
            sbParams.AppendFormat(",{0}", parameter.Key);
            sql.Replace(parameter.Value, sbParams.ToString());
            sbParams.Clear();
        }

        var paginationParameters = new DynamicParameters(parameters);
        paginationParameters.Add("skip", pageSize * pageIndex, DbType.Int32, direction: System.Data.ParameterDirection.InputOutput);
        paginationParameters.Add("total", dbType: DbType.Int64, direction: System.Data.ParameterDirection.Output);

        _connection.Open();
        using (var multi = _connection.QueryMultiple(sql.ToString(), paginationParameters))
        {
            var dataReader = multi.Read();
            var totalCount = paginationParameters.Get<long>("total");
            // You can use the totalCount to implement pagination logic as needed
            return dataReader;
        }
    }

    private string GetParameterName(string parameterName)
    {
        return $"p{parameterName}";
    }
}
```

This example demonstrates a QueryBuilder class that accepts dynamic SQL queries, parameters, and optional sorting, pagination, and filtering options. It uses Dapper's `QueryMultiple` method to execute the query and retrieve both the result set and total count of records. The method also includes a simple parameter naming convention for better readability and maintainability.

You can use this QueryBuilder class within your existing data access layer, ensuring backward compatibility and minimal changes to the current system. It's essential to optimize the SQL queries according to your specific database schema and performance requirements. Additionally, consider implementing caching strategies, connection pooling, and error handling mechanisms as needed for your application.']

