Here's a simple example for starting and stopping a service using Quartz Scheduler:

```java
import org.quartz.*;
import org.quartz.impl.StdSchedulerFactory;

public class ServiceManager {
    private Scheduler scheduler;

    public void init() throws SchedulerException {
        scheduler = StdSchedulerFactory.getDefaultScheduler();
        scheduler.start();
    }

    public void startService(String cronExpression) throws SchedulerException {
        JobDetail job = JobBuilder.newJob(ServiceStartJob.class)
                .withIdentity("service-start", "group1")
                .build();

        CronScheduleBuilder scheduleBuilder = CronScheduleBuilder.cronSchedule(cronExpression);
        Trigger trigger = TriggerBuilder.newTrigger()
                .withIdentity("service-start-trigger", "group1")
                .withSchedule(scheduleBuilder)
                .build();

        scheduler.scheduleJob(job, trigger);
    }

    public void stopService(String cronExpression) throws SchedulerException {
        JobDetail job = JobBuilder.newJob(ServiceStopJob.class)
                .withIdentity("service-stop", "group1")
                .build();

        CronScheduleBuilder scheduleBuilder = CronScheduleBuilder.cronSchedule(cronExpression);
        Trigger trigger = TriggerBuilder.newTrigger()
                .withIdentity("service-stop-trigger", "group1")
                .withSchedule(scheduleBuilder)
                .build();

        scheduler.scheduleJob(job, trigger);
    }
}
```']

[' Here's a simplified PHP script that addresses your requirements. This script uses the `php` built-in functions for JSON handling, along with the `Psr\Log\LoggerInterface` for logging and the `Predis` library for Redis-based caching.

First, install Predis using Composer:

