[" In this example, I'll provide a C++ function that uses a pseudo-random number generator (PRNG) to select an attack based on the given weighted probabilities and critical state conditions. The function integrates with an existing attack manager by calling its `SelectAttack` method when an attack is chosen.

To minimize latency, I've used a static variable to store the last cooldown time and ensured that the function only selects an attack if the current time exceeds the cooldown period. To optimize for memory usage and performance, I've avoided unnecessary data structures and focused on efficient code execution.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <random>

// Centralized attack manager interface
class AttackManager {
public:
    virtual void SelectAttack(int attackNumber) = 0;
};

// Global cooldown timer and attack probabilities
std::mt19937 rng(std::random_device{}());
const int ATTACK_WEIGHTS[] = {40, 35, 25};
const int NUM_ATTACKS = sizeof(ATTACK_WEIGHTS) / sizeof(ATTACK_WEIGHTS[0]);
static std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point lastCooldown;

void SelectAttack(AttackManager& attackManager) {
    auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    if (now - lastCooldown > 9s) {
        int totalWeight = std::accumulate(ATTACK_WEIGHTS, ATTACK_WEIGHTS + NUM_ATTACKS, 0);
        int criticalWeight = 70;
        int weightSum = totalWeight + criticalWeight;

        // Determine if in a critical state
        bool isCritical = rng() % weightSum < criticalWeight * totalWeight;

        // Calculate the random index based on weights and critical state
        int attackIndex = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < NUM_ATTACKS; ++i) {
            if (isCritical && rng() % weightSum < criticalWeight * ATTACK_WEIGHTS[i]) {
                attackIndex = NUM_ATTACKS - 1 - i;
                break;
            } else if (!isCritical && rng() % totalWeight < ATTACK_WEIGHTS[i]) {
                attackIndex = i;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Select the attack and update cooldown timer
        attackManager.SelectAttack(attackIndex);
        lastCooldown = now;
    }
}
```

This function can be integrated into an existing game framework by replacing the `AttackManager` class with the appropriate implementation. The function ensures that attacks are selected based on the given weighted probabilities and critical state conditions, while minimizing latency during the cooldown period."]

