[' In C++11, you can use `std::lock_guard` from the `<mutex>` header to manage locks safely. Here's an implementation of the `updateOrInsertObject` function using a `std::unordered_map` for fast lookups and a `std::mutex` for synchronization:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <mutex>
#include <utility>

struct CustomObject {
    std::string identifier;
    int value;
    std::string status;

    CustomObject(const std::string& id, int val, const std::string& stat)
        : identifier(id), value(val), status(stat) {}
};

std::mutex mtx;
std::unordered_map<std::string, std::shared_ptr<CustomObject>> objects;

void updateOrInsertObject(std::vector<std::shared_ptr<CustomObject>>& vec, const std::string& identifier, int threshold) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    if (auto it = objects.find(identifier); it != objects.end()) {
        it->second->value += threshold * 1.5;
    } else {
        auto newObj = std::make_shared<CustomObject>(identifier, 0, "newly added");
        objects[identifier] = newObj;
        vec.push_back(newObj);
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a `std::unordered_map` to store the `CustomObject` pointers for fast lookups and a `std::mutex` to synchronize access to the map when multiple threads are modifying it concurrently. The function takes care of memory management using `std::shared_ptr`.

The function minimizes lock contention by acquiring the lock only when updating or inserting an object, allowing other read operations to proceed without locks. This approach should provide good performance under heavy load while maintaining thread safety.']

