[" Here's a C++ implementation of a thread-safe, high-performance random number generator (RNG) using the Mersenne Twister algorithm within the `MathUtils` namespace and `RandomGenerator` class. The code uses the C++11 standard and the `<thread>`, `<mutex>`, and `<random>` libraries.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <random>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>

namespace MathUtils {

class RandomGenerator {
public:
    explicit RandomGenerator(unsigned long long seed) : mt_(seed), buffer_(), mutex_() {}

    void reseed(unsigned long long newSeed) {
        std::mt19937_64 newEngine(newSeed);
        std::generate_n(std::back_inserter(buffer_), buffer_.capacity(), [&]() { return newEngine(); });
    }

    int nextInt(int min, int max) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        if (buffer_.empty()) {
            std::generate_n(std::back_inserter(buffer_), buffer_.capacity(), [&]() { return mt_(mt_.min()); });
        }
        auto it = buffer_.begin();
        advance(it, min / (1ULL << 32) + max / (1ULL << 32));
        std::advance(it, (it[0] - min) >> 32);
        return static_cast<int>(it[0]);
    }

private:
    std::mt19937_64 mt_;
    std::vector<unsigned long long> buffer_;
    mutable std::mutex mutex_;
};

}  // namespace MathUtils
```

This implementation uses the Mersenne Twister algorithm, which is a popular and efficient random number generator. The `RandomGenerator` class takes a seed value during construction and provides a method to reseed the generator periodically. The `nextInt()` method generates a random integer within the given range using a buffer for caching generated numbers to minimize overhead.

The code uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when accessing the buffer, preventing race conditions in multi-threaded applications. The buffer size is not explicitly defined, but it can be adjusted based on your specific requirements and trade-offs between performance and memory usage.

In case of failures in external data sources providing initial RNG parameters, you may want to implement a fallback mechanism or default seed value to ensure the system remains robust. Additionally, this implementation supports integration with various data types and distributions by using the C++ standard library's random number generation capabilities."]

