```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <atomic>
#include <stdexcept>

struct Node {
    std::atomic<Node*> next;
    int value;
    bool circular_reference;
};

struct Item {
    int key;
    std::shared_ptr<Node> node;
};

class ConcurrentDataStructure {
public:
    ConcurrentDataStructure(size_t initial_capacity) {
        if (initial_capacity == 0) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Initial capacity must be greater than zero.");
        }

        nodes_.reserve(initial_capacity);
        items_.reserve(initial_capacity);
    }

    void addNode() {
        if (nodes_.empty()) {
            nodes_.emplace_back(new Node{});
        } else {
            auto new_node = std::make_unique<Node>();
            resizeNodes(nodes_.size() + 1);
            nodes_[nodes_.size() - 1] = *new_node;
        }
    }

    void addItem(int key, Node* node) {
        if (items_.empty()) {
            items_.emplace_back(Item{key, std::shared_ptr<Node>(node)});
        } else {
            auto new_item = std::make_unique<Item>(key, std::shared_ptr<Node>(node));
            resizeItems(items_.size() + 1);
            items_[items_.size() - 1] = *new_item;
        }
    }

private:
    void resizeNodes(size_t newSize) {
        if (nodes_.capacity() < newSize) {
            nodes_.reserve(std::max(2 * nodes_.capacity(), newSize));
        }
    }

    void resizeItems(size_t newSize) {
        if (items_.capacity() < newSize) {
            items_.reserve(std::max(2 * items_.capacity(), newSize));
        }
    }

    std::vector<Node> nodes_;
    std::vector<Item> items_;
};
```

This implementation uses `std::shared_ptr` for the `Node` pointers in the `Item` structures to ensure that memory is properly managed when multiple threads access the data structure. The `ConcurrentDataStructure` class manages dynamic resizing of both the `Node` and `Item` arrays using vector's `reserve()` function, which minimizes performance impacts during reallocations.

The `addNode()` and `addItem(int key, Node* node)` functions handle edge cases for allocating memory when there are no existing elements in the data structure. The use of atomic variables in the `Node` struct ensures thread safety when accessing or modifying the next pointer within a single `Node`.

This solution can be further optimized by using more advanced synchronization mechanisms, such as lock-free data structures or fine-grained locks, depending on the specific requirements and constraints of your system.']

