[' Here's a high-level outline of how you might structure your `DynamicImageProcessingPipeline` class in Java. Due to the complexity of the task, I won't be able to provide the full implementation, but I can give you an idea of what each component could look like:

```java
import org.yaml.snakeyaml.Yaml;
import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import javax.imageio.*;

public class DynamicImageProcessingPipeline {
    private final ExecutorService executor;
    private final ImageProcessor imageProcessor;
    private final Cache<String, BufferedImage> cache;
    private final Configuration configuration;

    public DynamicImageProcessingPipeline() {
        this.executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
            0, // Initial size
            Integer.MAX_VALUE, // Maximum size
            Long.MAX_VALUE, // Keep alive time
            TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS,
            new LinkedBlockingQueue<>());

        this.imageProcessor = new ImageProcessor();
        this.cache = new LruCache<>(1000); // Adjust size as needed

        try {
            Yaml yaml = new Yaml();
            Map<String, Object> config = (Map<String, Object>) yaml.load(new FileInputStream("config.yaml"));
            this.configuration = new Configuration(config);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Handle exception and use default configuration or throw an error
        }
    }

    public void processImages(List<String> imageSources, String targetFormat, List<Transformation> transformations) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        List<Future<BufferedImage>> futures = new ArrayList<>();

        for (String source : imageSources) {
            BufferedImage image = cache.get(source);
            if (image == null) {
                // Load image from disk and apply transformations
                ImageReader reader = ImageIO.getImageReadersByFormatName(source).next();
                reader.setInput(new FileInputStream(source));
                image = reader.read(0);
            }

            for (Transformation transformation : transformations) {
                image = transformation.apply(image, configuration);
            }

            BufferedImage outputImage = new ImageProcessor().saveAs(image, targetFormat, configuration);
            cache.put(source, outputImage);

            Callable<BufferedImage> callable = () -> outputImage;
            futures.add(executor.submit(callable));
        }

        for (Future<BufferedImage> future : futures) {
            future.get();
        }
    }

    // Add methods for monitoring, adjusting thread pool size, handling cache misses, etc.
}
```

This outline includes a basic structure for the class, including an executor service for concurrent processing, an image processor for applying transformations and saving images, a cache for storing frequently processed images, and a configuration manager for loading settings from a YAML file. The `processImages` method demonstrates how to process a list of images with given transformations and save them in the desired format.

You'll need to implement additional methods for handling thread pool management, cache eviction strategies, GPU acceleration, performance monitoring, and other features as specified in your task description.']

