[' Here is a C++ implementation using a hash table with separate chaining for handling key uniqueness and concurrent read/write operations using `std::mutex`. The data structure uses an array for efficient memory access patterns and a linked list (`std::list`) for fast insertions and deletions.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <list>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <mutex>
#include <cassert>
#include <new>

constexpr size_t MAX_RECORDS = 10000000;

struct Record {
    int key;
    long offset;
};

class RecordManager {
public:
    RecordManager(size_t size) {
        if (size > MAX_RECORDS) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Size exceeds the maximum allowed records");
        }

        data.resize(hashFunction(size));
        bucketSize = size / data.size();
    }

    ~RecordManager() {
        for (auto& bucket : data) {
            for (auto record : bucket) {
                delete record;
            }
            bucket.clear();
        }
        data.clear();
    }

    Record* insert(int key, long offset) {
        if (key < 1 || key > INT_MAX - 1) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Key must be within the range of an integer");
        }

        size_t index = hashFunction(key);
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutexes[index]);

        auto it = std::find_if(data[index].begin(), data[index].end(),
                               [&](const Record* record) { return record->key == key; });
        if (it != data[index].end()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Key already exists in the hash table");
        }

        Record* newRecord = new Record{key, offset};
        data[index].push_back(newRecord);
        return newRecord;
    }

    Record* find(int key) const {
        if (key < 1 || key > INT_MAX - 1) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Key must be within the range of an integer");
        }

        size_t index = hashFunction(key);
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutexes[index]);

        auto it = std::find_if(data[index].begin(), data[index].end(),
                               [&](const Record* record) { return record->key == key; });
        if (it == data[index].end()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Key not found in the hash table");
        }

        return *it;
    }

    void remove(int key) {
        if (key < 1 || key > INT_MAX - 1) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Key must be within the range of an integer");
        }

        size_t index = hashFunction(key);
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutexes[index]);

        auto it = std::find_if(data[index].begin(), data[index].end(),
                               [&](const Record* record) { return record->key == key; });
        if (it == data[index].end()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Key not found in the hash table");
        }

        data[index].erase(it);
    }

private:
    size_t hashFunction(size_t key) const {
        return key % data.size();
    }

    std::vector<std::list<Record*>> data;
    std::vector<std::mutex> mutexes(data.size());
    size_t bucketSize;
};
```

This implementation uses a hash table with separate chaining for handling key uniqueness and concurrent read/write operations. The `RecordManager` class takes a size parameter to initialize the data structure, which is an array of linked lists (`std::list<Record*>`). Each bucket in the array has a fixed number of records based on the total number of buckets.

The `insert`, `find`, and `remove` functions use a mutex for each bucket to protect against race conditions during concurrent operations. The hash function is simple, using modulo arithmetic to determine the index of a record in the array.

The `RecordManager` class also handles memory management by dynamically allocating and deallocating records as needed. It uses `new` and `delete` for memory allocation and provides a destructor to clean up resources when the object is destroyed.

This implementation should be adaptable to future changes, as it separates the hash table's data structure from the concurrency control mechanisms. The interface is simple, with clear documentation for usage and expected behavior. Edge cases and potential failure scenarios are handled gracefully by throwing exceptions.']

