[' Here's a simplified implementation of the described Java service using the `Executors` library for managing threads, `Apache Commons Codec` for SHA-256 hashing, and `Caffeine` for the thread-safe in-memory cache. This example does not include external logging integration or dynamic adjustment of processing parameters, but it provides a good starting point.

```java
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Cache;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Caffeine;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class MessageProcessingService {
    private final Cache<String, String> messageCache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
            .maximumSize(10_000)
            .expireAfterAccess(1, TimeUnit.HOURS) // Adjust expiration time as needed
            .build((key, value) -> cleanAndHashMessage(value));
    private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(20);

    public void processMessage(String message) {
        String cleanedAndHashedMessage = messageCache.getIfPresent(message);
        if (cleanedAndHashedMessage == null) {
            Runnable task = () -> messageCache.putIfAbsent(message, cleanAndHashMessage(message));
            executor.submit(task);
        }
    }

    private String cleanAndHashMessage(String message) {
        return DigestUtils.sha256Hex(message.replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z0-9]", ""));
    }
}
```

This code creates a `MessageProcessingService` that processes incoming messages by cleaning and hashing them, caching the results in a thread-safe cache with a capacity of 10,000 entries. The service uses a fixed thread pool to manage up to 20 concurrent message processing threads. To handle temporary spikes in message volume, consider implementing backpressure mechanisms such as throttling or queueing messages when the cache is full or the executor is saturated.

To integrate with an external logging system, you can use a library like `Log4j` or `Slf4j` to log messages at various levels (e.g., INFO, WARN, ERROR) based on the status of each message and report errors or delays. To dynamically adjust processing parameters, you can monitor system health metrics (e.g., cache hit rate, thread pool utilization, response times) and modify the concurrency limits, timeout thresholds, and cache expiration settings accordingly.

For cache eviction policies, consider using LRU (Least Recently Used) or ARC (Adaptive Replacement Cache) strategies to minimize data loss or corruption during peak loads while maintaining good performance.']

