The doubly-linked list design helps minimize memory overhead by allowing for efficient insertion and deletion operations without requiring extensive reallocation of memory. Additionally, the use of pointers to dynamically allocated char arrays for identifiers ensures that each identifier is unique, while the cache performance can be optimized by carefully managing the order of entries in the list (e.g., keeping frequently accessed entries close together).']

