[' Here's a simplified C++ example that addresses some of the design challenges mentioned. This code uses the `std::vector` container for dynamic memory management, `std::thread` for multi-threading, and `std::mutex` for thread safety. The example also includes a basic strategy for handling inconsistent data feeds and buffering data during delays.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <random>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <queue>

struct sensor_data {
    double temperature;
    float humidity;
    float pressure;
};

std::mutex data_mutex;
std::condition_variable cv;
std::queue<sensor_data> sensor_data_queue;

void acquireData(int sensor_id) {
    std::random_device rd;
    std::mt19937 gen(rd());
    std::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 100); // temperature range: [0, 100]

    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(data_mutex);
    sensor_data new_data;
    new_data.temperature = dis(gen);
    new_data.humidity = static_cast<float>(dis(gen)); // humidity range: [0, 100]
    new_data.pressure = static_cast<float>(dis(gen)); // pressure range: [0, 1000]

    sensor_data_queue.push(new_data);
    cv.notify_one();
}

void processData(int thread_id) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(data_mutex);
    cv.wait(lock, []{ return !sensor_data_queue.empty(); });

    auto data = sensor_data_queue.front();
    sensor_data_queue.pop();
    lock.unlock();

    // Process the data here
    std::cout << "Thread " << thread_id << " processed temperature: " << data.temperature << "\n";
}

void run(int num_threads, int num_sensors) {
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    std::vector<sensor_data> sensor_data_records(num_sensors);

    for (int i = 0; i < num_threads; ++i) {
        threads.emplace_back(processData, i + 1);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < num_sensors; ++i) {
        acquireData(i);
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(rand() % 500)); // simulate data acquisition delay
    }

    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        thread.join();
    }
}

int main() {
    run(4, 1000);
    return 0;
}
```

This example demonstrates a simple multi-threaded solution that can handle large numbers of sensors and data records. It uses a queue to buffer data during delays in data acquisition and employs condition variables for efficient waiting on the queue. The code also includes a basic strategy for handling inconsistent data feeds by simply discarding any data that arrives after all threads have been started.

To further optimize this solution, consider implementing more sophisticated strategies for handling inconsistent data feeds, such as retrying failed acquisitions or using exponential backoff to manage retries. Additionally, you may want to explore concurrent data structures like `std::concurrent_queue` and other synchronization mechanisms like `std::atomic` for even better performance in a multi-threaded context.']

